  "query_preview": "..\n\nRest of Today through Wednesday...\n\nA Flood Watch has been issued for all except Fallon, Custer, \nand Carter Counties, from Friday evening through the day Sunday.\n\nForecast trending more unsettled this afternoon through this\nweekend, with the flood potential increasing for central and\nwestern portions of the forecast area. The area is currently under\nsoutherly diffluent flow wrapping around a deepening upper low\nover the CA/NV/OR triple point. At the surface southeasterly \nwinds gusting to 30 mph over southeast MT are pulling substantial \nplains moisture into the area. By this evening, Precipitable Water\nvalues will be pushing 4 standard deviation above normal (220% of\nnormal) over SE Montana, and by Saturday morning values of \n220-240% of normal will be widespread across the forecast area. \nThe upper low to the west will drift slowly this direction over \nthe next 36 hours and then track across central Wyoming this \nweekend, exiting to the east on Monday. This track is the best \nprecipitation pattern for our area and combined with the high \natmospheric moisture values is primed to bring at least locally \nheavy rainfall to the region, if not areas of heavy rainfall. The \nlatest model runs have come into very good agreement with the \nstorm track and precipitation intensity. \n\nNBM 48hr probabilities for over 1 inch of precipitation Friday \nnight through Sunday are in the 80-100% range for most of the \nforecast area, with 2 inch probabilities of 50-70% from Rosebud \ncounty west into the foothills. The NBM mean precipitation for the\nsame 48 hour period ranges from 1.7 inches in Livingston, 2.3 \ninches in Columbus, 2.2 inches in Billings, 1.9 inches \nHardin/Colstrip, and 1.6 inches in Sheridan. Going to the high end\nof the NBM (90th percentile) brings in areas of 3 to 4 inches of \nrainfall, likely in areas where thunderstorms develop and tap \ninto that high atmospheric moisture. As a result of the above have\nissued a Flood Watch for the weekend. The mean rainfall over a \ncouple of days is probably only a minor impact, but the higher \npotential that could double those mean values in some areas would \nbe a higher impact flood scenario. \n\nRecent burn scars will be highly susceptible for flash flooding\nand debris flows in the current long duration precipitation\nforecast, especially if thunderstorm precipitation impacts them.\nThose living near recent burn scars should have a plan of action\nto seek safety should the heavy rainfall develop in those\nlocations. Pay attention to local officials directions and for any\nFlash Flood Warnings that may be issued. \n\nThere still looks to be a window for a few stronger thunderstorms\non Friday afternoon/evening for areas from Billings east into SE\nMontana. Shear profiles do not look as strong as they did\nyesterday, but with sun early in the day allowing temperatures\ninto the lower 80s along with the strong PWATs, CAPE values will\nbe supportive of hail and gusty winds to go along with very heavy\nrainfall. Those with outdoor and sports viewing plans should pay\nattention to the forecast and be prepared in case a strong storm\ndevelops in your vicinity tomorrow. \n\nPrecipitation should taper off Sunday night into Monday from west\nto east as the upper low moves into the Northern Plains states.\nWhile much drier weather is forecast for next week, depending on\nhow much rain falls this weekend, there will probably be a good\nchance for at least afternoon and evening showers and\nthunderstorms each day into mid-week just from the residual\nsurface moisture across the region. \n\nSnowfall...With the increased precipitation forecast, snow\namounts in the highest peaks of area mountains have climbed into\nthe 3 to 6 inch range. Snow levels start out around 12kft Friday\nnight, dropping to 11kft Saturday afternoon and possibly falling\nto around 10kft Saturday night, which is a bit lower than\nprevious forecasts. May still need a winter weather advisory for\nthe Beartooth Pass with this system, but looks like it would be\nSaturday night into Sunday morning, so will hold off on that for\nnow. That said, anyone planning travel over the pass this weekend\nshould be prepared for winter conditions. \n\nTemperatures will reach the 75 to 85 degree range on Friday,\nwarmest over the east. Highs will be mainly in the 60s with the\nwidespread precipitation Sat/Sun. 70s will be common the rest of\nnext week. Chambers",
